{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=\\\"/tmp/gh-aw/agent/issues_index.json\\\"; d=json.load(open(p)); print(type(d).__name__); rows=d if isinstance(d,list) else d.get(\\\"issues\\\", []); nums={3891,370,2176,3701,3284}; [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)) for x in rows if x.get(\\\"number\\\") in nums]'\"","aggregated_output":"list\n{\n  \"number\": 370,\n  \"title\": \"[REQUEST] print: print every arguments on the same line\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2020-10-09T16:38:30Z\",\n  \"closed_at\": \"2020-10-09T18:06:28Z\",\n  \"state_reason\": \"completed\",\n  \"body\": \"Have you checked the issues for a similar suggestions? Yes\\r\\n\\r\\n**How would you improve Rich?**\\r\\n\\r\\nHi,\\r\\n\\r\\nIs it possible to print all values given to `rich.print` including data structures on the same line?\\r\\n\\r\\nExample:\\r\\n```python\\r\\nfrom rich import print\\r\\nprint('a', {'b': 'c'})\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\noutput:\\r\\n```\\r\\na\\r\\n{'b': 'c'}\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nIs it possible to make it outputs\\r\\n```\\r\\na {'b': 'c'}\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nOf course while still highlighting `{'b': 'c'}`.\\r\\n\\r\\nI have tried setting `soft_wrap`, etc. with no effect.\\r\\n\\r\\n**What problem does it solved for you?**\\r\\n\\r\\nIt is much easier to scan for the output if they are put on the same line, when there are multiple outputs lines.\\r\\n\\r\\nExample\\r\\n```\\r\\nINSERT {'value': 100}\\r\\nDELETE {'value': 200}\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nCompared to\\r\\n```\\r\\nINSERT\\r\\n{'value': 100}\\r\\nDELETE\\r\\n{'value': 200}\\r\\n```\\r\\n\"\n}\n{\n  \"number\": 2176,\n  \"title\": \"Prevent context manager to overwrite previous one in jupyter\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2022-04-07T13:29:39Z\",\n  \"closed_at\": \"2024-07-02T14:06:21Z\",\n  \"state_reason\": \"completed\",\n  \"body\": \"When in Jupyter lab, if I call independent instances of a `Progress()`, for example by mapping a function that invokes a `Progress()` through an iterable, the context manager overwrites/deletes previous instances along the iteration. \\r\\n\\r\\nFor example (inside a Jupyter Lab notebook) inspired from code in the tutorial:\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\nimport time\\r\\n\\r\\nfrom rich.progress import Progress\\r\\n\\r\\ndef run_snippet(iteration):\\r\\n    with Progress() as progress:\\r\\n\\r\\n        task1 = progress.add_task(\\\"[red]Downloading...\\\", total=5)\\r\\n        while not progress.finished:\\r\\n            progress.update(task1, advance=0.5)\\r\\n            #progress.log(f'this iteration {iteration}') <- uncomment for \\\"in-context\\\" effect\\r\\n            time.sleep(0.2)\\r\\n            \\r\\nlist(map(run_snippet, range(10)))\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nThe code above will overwrite any print-out (or anything else like plots or rich.renderables) from previous iterations … even within the same context (for example by un-commenting the `.log()` line ). This behavior doesn't happen when executed from a console. There, anything that is printed or rendered, remains visible and piles up.\\r\\n\\r\\nMy ignorant guess is that when the next `Process()` context is invoked, the way jupyter handles it by just throwing clearing away what was displayed in the past and moves forward.\\r\\n\\r\\nIs there a way to prevent his?\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nAnd finally, thanks so much for this wonderful tool!\\r\\n\\r\\n(Edited some words for clarity.)\\r\\n(Edit2: updated to specify that case has been tested only on Jupyter lab instead of Jupyter notebook)\"\n}\n{\n  \"number\": 3284,\n  \"title\": \"Is there a way to print the line number while console.log'ging a single line?\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2024-02-22T22:20:12Z\",\n  \"closed_at\": \"2024-02-22T22:33:02Z\",\n  \"state_reason\": \"completed\",\n  \"body\": \"I was recommended rich's `console.log()` as a way to add to a `print` statement output the number of the line of the `print` statement itself. With `console.log(some_array)` the number of it's line is indeed being printed — after a column next to filename. But when I `console.log()` a single string there is no line number being printed. Is there a way to print one?\"\n}\n{\n  \"number\": 3701,\n  \"title\": \"[BUG] Behavior of console.print() with empty *args is different from regular print()\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2025-04-15T22:02:12Z\",\n  \"closed_at\": null,\n  \"state_reason\": null,\n  \"body\": \"**Describe the bug**\\n\\nExpected that `console.print(end=\\\"!\\\")` will print only `!` as regular `python print()`, but it print line break without ending symbol.\\n\\n```python\\nfrom rich.console import Console\\n\\nconsole = Console()\\n\\nfor f in (\\n    lambda: print(\\\"A\\\", end=\\\"!\\\"),\\n    lambda: print(end=\\\"!\\\"),\\n    lambda: console.print(\\\"A\\\", end=\\\"!\\\"),\\n    lambda: console.print(end=\\\"!\\\"),\\n):\\n    print(\\\"=>\\\", end=\\\"\\\")\\n    f()\\n    print(\\\"<=\\\")\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n```\\n❯ python3 test.py\\n=>A!<=\\n=>!<=\\n=>A!<=\\n=>\\n<=\\n```\\n\\nrich == 14.0.0\"\n}\n{\n  \"number\": 3891,\n  \"title\": \"[BUG] print() return value is error。\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2025-11-19T05:50:33Z\",\n  \"closed_at\": \"2025-12-01T20:46:07Z\",\n  \"state_reason\": \"completed\",\n  \"body\": \"```python\\nfrom rich import print\\n```\\nReplace built-in print function  to  rich.print\\n\\nwhen obj is complex objects like \\n```python\\nfrom datetime import date\\nd = date.fromisoformat(\\\"2025-08-09\\\")\\nl = [[d,1,1],[d,2,2]]\\n```\\n_The return value of the built-in print function is_\\n```python\\n>>> print(f\\\"ok {l}\\\")\\nok [[datetime.date(2025, 8, 9), 1, 1], [datetime.date(2025, 8, 9), 2, 2]]\\n```\\n**the return value of the rich.print is** \\n```python\\n>>> from rich import print\\n>>> print(f\\\"ok {l}\\\")\\nok [, ]\\n```\\nno f-string ,only print is right\\n```python\\n>>> print(l)\\n[[datetime.date(2025, 8, 9), 1, 1], [datetime.date(2025, 8, 9), 2, 2]]\\n```\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
