{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -i \\\"carriage return|overwrite|\\\\bflush\\\\b|\\\\r|Console\\\\.print|rich\\\\.print\\\" docs/source rich/__init__.py rich/console.py\"","aggregated_output":"rich/__init__.py:58:    flush: bool = False,\nrich/__init__.py:68:        flush (bool, optional): Has no effect as Rich always flushes output. Defaults to False.\nrich/__init__.py:74:    return write_console.print(*objects, sep=sep, end=end)\nrich/__init__.py:173:    _console.print(_inspect)\ndocs/source/tables.rst:25:    console.print(table)\ndocs/source/introduction.rst:72:If you would rather not shadow Python's built-in print, you can import ``rich.print`` as ``rprint`` (for example)::\ndocs/source/highlighting.rst:8:You can disable highlighting either by setting ``highlight=False`` on :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`, or by setting ``highlight=False`` on the :class:`~rich.console.Console` constructor which disables it everywhere. If you disable highlighting on the constructor, you can still selectively *enable* highlighting with ``highlight=True`` on print / log.\ndocs/source/highlighting.rst:30:    console.print(\"Send funds to money@example.org\")\ndocs/source/highlighting.rst:40:    console.print(highlight_emails(\"Send funds to money@example.org\"))\nrich/console.py:436:        self.console.print(self.screen, end=\"\")\nrich/console.py:1110:            ...     console.print(\"[bold magenta]Hello World[/]\")\nrich/console.py:1135:                    console.print(make_test_card())\nrich/console.py:1625:        :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` won't pretty print, wrap text, or apply markup, but will\nrich/console.py:2116:                    self.file.flush()\nrich/console.py:2651:    console.print(\ndocs/source/syntax.rst:14:    console.print(syntax)\ndocs/source/syntax.rst:24:    console.print(syntax)\ndocs/source/traceback.rst:14:The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_exception` method will print a traceback for the current exception being handled. Here's an example::\ndocs/source/traceback.rst:22:        console.print_exception(show_locals=True)\ndocs/source/traceback.rst:64:If you are working with a framework (click, django etc), you may only be interested in seeing the code from your own application within the traceback. You can exclude framework code by setting the `suppress` argument on `Traceback`, `install`, `Console.print_exception`, and `RichHandler`, which should be a list of modules or str paths.\ndocs/source/traceback.rst:97:        console.print_exception(max_frames=20)\ndocs/source/progress.rst:200:            progress.console.print(f\"Working on job #{job}\")\ndocs/source/progress.rst:209:        my_console.print(\"[bold blue]Starting work!\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:48:To write rich content to the terminal use the :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` method. Rich will convert any object to a string via its (``__str__``) method and perform some simple syntax highlighting. It will also do pretty printing of any containers, such as dicts and lists. If you print a string it will render :ref:`console_markup`. Here are some examples::\ndocs/source/console.rst:50:    console.print([1, 2, 3])\ndocs/source/console.rst:51:    console.print(\"[blue underline]Looks like a link\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:52:    console.print(locals())\ndocs/source/console.rst:53:    console.print(\"FOO\", style=\"white on blue\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:55:You can also use :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` to render objects that support the :ref:`protocol`, which includes Rich's built-in objects such as :class:`~rich.text.Text`, :class:`~rich.table.Table`, and :class:`~rich.syntax.Syntax` -- or other custom objects.\ndocs/source/console.rst:76:The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method will pretty print (format and style) a string containing JSON. Here's a short example::\ndocs/source/console.rst:78:    console.print_json('[false, true, null, \"foo\"]')\ndocs/source/console.rst:97:In additional to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` and :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`, Rich has an :meth:`~rich.console.Console.out` method which provides a lower-level way of writing to the terminal. The out() method converts all the positional arguments to strings and won't pretty print, word wrap, or apply markup to the output, but can apply a basic style and will optionally do highlighting.\ndocs/source/console.rst:152:    console.print(\"Rich\", style=style)\ndocs/source/console.rst:153:    console.print(\"Rich\", style=style, justify=\"left\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:154:    console.print(\"Rich\", style=style, justify=\"center\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:155:    console.print(\"Rich\", style=style, justify=\"right\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:173:You can specify how Rich should handle overflow with the ``overflow`` argument to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` which should be one of the following strings: \"fold\", \"crop\", \"ellipsis\", or \"ignore\". The default is \"fold\" which will put any excess characters on the following line, creating as many new lines as required to fit the text.\ndocs/source/console.rst:190:        console.print(supercali, overflow=overflow, style=\"bold blue\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:191:        console.print()\ndocs/source/console.rst:210:You can also set overflow to \"ignore\" which allows text to run on to the next line. In practice this will look the same as \"crop\" unless you also set ``crop=False`` when calling :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print`.\ndocs/source/console.rst:220:    blue_console.print(\"I'm blue. Da ba dee da ba di.\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:226:Rich word wraps text you print by inserting line breaks. You can disable this behavior by setting ``soft_wrap=True`` when calling :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print`. With *soft wrapping* enabled any text that doesn't fit will run on to the following line(s), just like the built-in ``print``.\ndocs/source/console.rst:232:The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` method has a boolean ``crop`` argument. The default value for crop is True which tells Rich to crop any content that would otherwise run on to the next line. You generally don't need to think about cropping, as Rich will resize content to fit within the available width.\ndocs/source/console.rst:321:        console.print(\"[bold red]Hello[/] World\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:329:    console.print(\"[bold red]Hello[/] World\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:344:        console.print(make_test_card())\ndocs/source/console.rst:366:        console.print(locals())\ndocs/source/markup.rst:6:Rich supports a simple markup which you can use to insert color and styles virtually everywhere Rich would accept a string (e.g. :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` and :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`).\ndocs/source/markup.rst:72:        console.print(f\"Hello {name}!\")\ndocs/source/markup.rst:78:        console.print(f\"Hello {escape(name)}!\")\ndocs/source/markup.rst:103:By default, Rich will render console markup when you explicitly pass a string to :meth:`~rich.console.Print.print` or implicitly when you embed a string in another renderable object such as :class:`~rich.table.Table` or :class:`~rich.panel.Panel`.\ndocs/source/markup.rst:105:Console markup is convenient, but you may wish to disable it if the syntax clashes with the string you want to print. You can do this by setting ``markup=False`` on the :meth:`~rich.console.Print.print` method or on the :class:`~rich.console.Console` constructor.\ndocs/source/conf.py:68:# so a file named \"default.css\" will overwrite the builtin \"default.css\".\ndocs/source/logging.rst:56:If you are working with a framework (click, django etc), you may only be interested in seeing the code from your own application within the traceback. You can exclude framework code by setting the `suppress` argument on `Traceback`, `install`, and `Console.print_exception`, which should be a list of modules or str paths.\ndocs/source/markdown.rst:19:    console.print(md)\ndocs/source/protocol.rst:7:Rich supports a simple protocol to add rich formatting capabilities to custom objects, so you can :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` your object with color, styles and formatting.\ndocs/source/live.rst:126:            live.console.print(f\"Working on row #{row}\")\ndocs/source/live.rst:136:        my_console.print(\"[bold blue]Starting work!\")\ndocs/source/text.rst:18:    console.print(text)\ndocs/source/text.rst:27:    console.print(text)\ndocs/source/text.rst:32:    console.print(text.spans)\ndocs/source/text.rst:37:    console.print(text)\ndocs/source/style.rst:17:    console.print(\"Hello\", style=\"magenta\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:21:    console.print(\"Hello\", style=\"color(5)\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:25:    console.print(\"Hello\", style=\"#af00ff\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:26:    console.print(\"Hello\", style=\"rgb(175,0,255)\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:35:    console.print(\"DANGER!\", style=\"red on white\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:59:    console.print(\"Danger, Will Robinson!\", style=\"blink bold red underline on white\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:63:    console.print(\"foo [not bold]bar[/not bold] baz\", style=\"bold\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:71:    console.print(\"Google\", style=\"link https://google.com\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:85:    console.print(\"Danger, Will Robinson!\", style=danger_style)\ndocs/source/style.rst:96:    console.print(\"Hello, World\", style = base_style + Style(underline=True))\ndocs/source/style.rst:123:    console.print(\"This is information\", style=\"info\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:124:    console.print(\"[warning]The pod bay doors are locked[/warning]\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:125:    console.print(\"Something terrible happened!\", style=\"danger\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:140:    console.print(\"The total is 128\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
